@page "/guide"

<PageTitle>CardGameEngine — Step-by-step guide</PageTitle>

<MudContainer MaxWidth="MaxWidth.Large" Class="py-8">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h3">Step-by-step guide</MudText>
    <MudText Class="mt-2 mb-6">
        Follow these steps to move from an empty .NET project to a playable card-game prototype.
        Each step explains the purpose of the API and shows a minimal example you can adapt to your game.
    </MudText>

    <MudStepper Vertical="true">
        <MudStep Title="1. Install CardGameEngine">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Add the published framework package to your .NET 10 application. CGE is intentionally
                independent from the application UI, so the same package can be used from console,
                desktop, mobile or server applications.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>dotnet new console -n MyCardGame
cd MyCardGame
dotnet add package CardGameEngine --version 1.0.0</code></pre>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="2. Create players and game">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Create the participants and give the engine ownership of the game state. A Game contains
                the players, active player and the state required by the action pipeline.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>using CardGameEngine;

var player = new Player();
var opponent = new Player();

var game = new Game(new List&lt;IPlayer&gt;
{
    player,
    opponent
});

game.StartGame(
    initialHandSize: 3,
    initialPlayerLife: 20);</code></pre>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="3. Build your deck">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Populate each player's deck with the cards that belong to your game. The engine manages
                the deck as part of the player state; your application decides which cards exist and how
                a deck is assembled.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>var knight = new KnightCard();
var fireball = new FireballCard();

player.Deck.Add(knight);
player.Deck.Add(fireball);

// Add the cards required by your game rules before starting the match.</code></pre>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                Keep deck construction in your game/application layer. CardGameEngine supplies the
                containers and execution rules, not a fixed deck-building strategy.
            </MudAlert>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="4. Create monster cards">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Define creatures that occupy board slots and can participate in combat. Derive your
                game-specific card from <code>MonsterCard</code> and provide the statistics and behavior
                required by your game.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>public sealed class KnightCard : MonsterCard
{
    // Configure the card's name, mana cost, attack and life
    // according to the MonsterCard API used by your game.
}

// A monster is played through the game/player action API:
player.CastMonster(game, knight, boardIndex: 0);</code></pre>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                The cast action validates that the player is active, owns the card, can summon it and
                has a free board slot before changing the state.
            </MudAlert>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="5. Create spells">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Choose between a spell with no selected target and one that requires a target.
                Use <code>TargetlessSpellCard</code> for effects such as healing yourself, and
                <code>TargetfulSpellCard</code> for effects such as damaging a selected character.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>// No target:
public sealed class HealCard : TargetlessSpellCard
{
    // Compose the card with a TargetlessSpellCardComponent.
}

// With a target:
public sealed class FireballCard : TargetfulSpellCard
{
    // Compose the card with a TargetfulSpellCardComponent.
}</code></pre>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="6. Implement components">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Put reusable card effects into components instead of coupling the effect directly to the
                game loop. Components can execute normal engine actions, while targetful components also
                calculate valid targets from the current <code>IGameState</code>.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>public sealed class HealComponent : TargetlessSpellCardComponent
{
    // When the spell resolves:
    game.Execute(
        new ModifyLifeStatAction(
            game.ActivePlayer,
            3));
}

public sealed class FireballComponent : TargetfulSpellCardComponent
{
    public override HashSet&lt;ICharacter&gt; GetPotentialTargets(
        IGameState gameState)
    {
        var targets = new HashSet&lt;ICharacter&gt;();

        foreach (var player in gameState.NonActivePlayers)
        {
            foreach (var character in player.Characters)
            {
                targets.Add(character);
            }
        }

        return targets;
    }
}</code></pre>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                The target set should be calculated from the current game state. Do not keep a stale list
                of targets that may no longer be valid when the action executes.
            </MudAlert>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="7. Use actions">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Treat actions as the units of state transition. Execute them through
                <code>Game.Execute(...)</code> so validation, events and reactions remain part of the
                same pipeline.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>// Draw a card:
game.Execute(new DrawCardAction(player));

// Move to the next turn:
game.NextTurn();</code></pre>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Warning" Class="mt-4">
                Avoid directly changing engine-owned collections or statistics. Put preconditions in
                <code>IsExecutable(IGameState)</code> and let the engine evaluate them immediately before execution.
            </MudAlert>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="8. Add reactions">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Reactions implement automatic rules triggered by actions or lifecycle events. A reaction
                can return additional actions, which are processed through the same execution pipeline.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>public sealed class DrawReaction : IReaction
{
    public IEnumerable&lt;IAction&gt; React(
        IAction action,
        IGameState gameState)
    {
        if (action is not DrawCardAction)
            return [];

        // Return additional actions required by your rule.
        return [];
    }
}</code></pre>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                Use events to expose meaningful lifecycle points, and reactions to attach automatic rules
                to those points without hard-coding every rule into the main game loop.
            </MudAlert>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="9. Run the game">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Start the turn lifecycle and perform legal actions. CGE validates the action against the
                current state, then processes the resulting events and reactions.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>game.NextTurn();

player.CastMonster(
    game,
    knight,
    boardIndex: 0);

player.CastSpell(
    game,
    fireball);</code></pre>
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Info" Class="mt-4">
                Combat follows the same principle: the attacker must belong to the active player's board,
                be ready to attack and have the selected target among its valid targets.
            </MudAlert>
        </MudStep>

        <MudStep Title="10. Validate and test">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Objective</MudText>
            <MudText Class="mb-4">
                Verify your rules against both normal and invalid game states. The action pipeline is
                designed to make these invariants explicit and testable.
            </MudText>
            <pre><code>dotnet build CGE.slnx -c Release
dotnet test CGE.slnx -c Release

dotnet run --project examples/CardGameEngine.Demo/CardGameEngine.Demo.csproj</code></pre>
            <MudText Class="mt-4">
                Add regression tests for your own rules, especially cases where a player is not active,
                a card is not in the hand, a board slot is occupied, a hand is full or a target is no
                longer valid.
            </MudText>
        </MudStep>
    </MudStepper>
</MudContainer>
